{%- case gem_handle -%}

  {%- when 'navratana' -%}
  <div class="gem-tab gem-tab--navratana">
    <h2>What Belongs in Navratna</h2>
    <b>The Nine Gems of Navratna</b>
    <ul>
      <p>☉ Ruby — Vitality & Purpose</p>
      <p>☽ Pearl — Emotional Balance</p>
      <p>♂ Red Coral — Courage & Action</p>
      <p>☿ Emerald — Clarity & Communication</p>
      <p>♃ Yellow Sapphire — Wisdom & Growth</p>
      <p>♀ White Sapphire / Diamond — Harmony & Refinement</p>
      <p>♄ Blue Sapphire — Discipline & Stability</p>
      <p>☊ Hessonite — Discernment & Protection</p>
      <p>☋ Cat's Eye — Focus & Precision</p>
    </ul>
    <ul>
    <p>Each gemstone contributes its own presence.</p>
    <p>Together, they form one of the oldest and most recognised gemstone combinations in Vedic tradition.</p>
    </ul>
  </div>

  {%- when 'upratnas' -%}
  <div class="gem-tab gem-tab--upratnas">
    <h2>What Belongs in Upratnas</h2>
    <b>The Traditional Upratnas</b>
    <ul>
      <p>A number of gemstones have traditionally been associated with specific planetary energies and primary Navratna gemstones.</p>
      <p>Examples include Moonstone for Pearl, Citrine (Sunela) for Yellow Sapphire, Iolite (Kakaneeli) for Blue Sapphire, Natural Zircon (Jarkan) for Diamond, Red Garnet for Ruby, Carnelian for Red Coral, Peridot for Emerald, Cinnamon Stone Garnet for Hessonite, and Quartz Cat’s Eye for Cat’s Eye Chrysoberyl.</p>
      <p>These relationships may vary among astrologers and traditions, but all share a common principle: offering a recognised and natural gemstone associated with a particular planetary influence.</p>
      <p>Each stone carries its own beauty, identity, and history while remaining connected to a broader astrological framework.</p>
    </ul>
  </div>

 {%- when 'beyond-nine' -%}
<div class="gem-tab gem-tab--beyond_nine">
  <h2>What Belongs in Beyond Nine</h2>
  <p>Nature's Rarest Expressions</p>
  <p>Beyond Nine may include:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Alexandrite</li>
    <li>Colour-Change Sapphire</li>
    <li>Padparadscha Sapphire</li>
    <li>Fine Spinel</li>
    <li>Tanzanite</li>
    <li> Imperial Topaz</li>
    <li>Demantoid Garnet</li>
    <li>Other exceptional natural gemstones</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Many of these stones are celebrated for rare colours, unusual optical phenomena, limited availability, or remarkable geological origins.</p>
  <p>No two stories are exactly alike</p>
  <p>And that is precisely the point.</p>
</div>


{%- when 'sacred-essentials' -%}
<div class="gem-tab gem-tab--sacred_essentials">
  <h2>What Belongs in Sacred Essentials</h2>
  <p>A gemstone enters Sacred Essentials because it meets our standards—not because it is inexpensive.</p>
  <b>Sacred Essentials may include:</b>
  <ul>
    <li>Natural authenticity</li>
    <li>Astrological suitability</li>
    <li>Treatment transparency</li>
    <li>Wearability and durability</li>
    <li>Honest value</li>
  </ul>
  <p>These are gemstones selected to make belief more accessible while preserving trust.</p>
</div>

